# ApexSenseBridge: hide the virtual DualSense from SDL, keep the hidraw path.
#
# Without this a game sees the pad twice - once through Proton's SDL backend and
# once through its hidraw one - and the prompts flicker between PlayStation and
# Xbox on every press. SDL reaches the pad by two routes and this rule closes
# only the evdev one; the other, SDL's own HIDAPI PS5 driver reading
# /dev/hidraw* directly, is closed by SDL_JOYSTICK_HIDAPI_PS5=0 in the game's
# environment. Both are needed.
#
# Clearing ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK alone does not work: when udev says nothing, SDL
# guesses the device class from the capability bits and finds a joystick anyway.
# Denying access to the nodes is what actually hides them.
#
# The /dev/hidraw* node is deliberately untouched - that is the one Proton's
# hidraw backend reads, and it carries the DualSense identity and the adaptive
# triggers.
#
# Note this also applies to a real DualSense plugged into the same machine,
# which will likewise stop appearing as a joystick to SDL clients.

SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{id/vendor}=="054c", ATTRS{id/product}=="0ce6", \
  ENV{ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK}="", ENV{ID_INPUT_ACCELEROMETER}="", \
  TAG-="uaccess", MODE="0600", OWNER="root", GROUP="root"
